.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt QUARK-MON 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm quark-mon
.Nd monitor and print quark events
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm quark-mon
.Op Fl BbDEeFGgHhkLMNnSsTtuv
.Op Fl C Ar filename
.Op Fl K Ar kubeconfig
.Op Fl l Ar maxlength
.Op Fl m Ar maxnodes
.Op Fl P Ar ppid
.Op Fl r Ar rulefile
.Nm quark-mon
.Fl h
.Nm quark-mon Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
program listens to all incoming
.Xr quark 7
events and prints them to stdout, the output format used is the one in
.Xr quark_event_dump 3 .
.Pp
The main utility is for testing and debugging quark, it's also a good
introduction to the code base.
.Nm
runs until a SIGINT is received.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Dtb
.It Fl B
Test bypass mode, where EBPF events are passed up directly without any
processing.
A
.Em *
is printed for each event.
.It Fl b
Attempt EBPF as the backend.
.It Fl C Ar filename
Dumps the contents of the internal cache into
.Ar filename
after exit.
Format is a
.Em dot
file that can be compiled to a
.Em svg
with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dot -Tsvg filename -o filename.svg
.Ed
.It Fl D
Drop priviledges to nobody and chroot to /var/empty, useful to show how quark
can run without priviledges.
.It Fl E
Output ECS JSON to stdout (experimental).
.It Fl e
Include
.Em proc_entry_leader
and
.Em proc_entry_type
in
.Em quark_events .
Entry leader is how the process entered the system, it is disabled by default as
it is Elastic/ECS specific.
.It Fl F
Enable file events (experimental).
.It Fl G
Enables a getpid probe, only for benchmarking,
.Em don't
use this otherwise!
.It Fl g
Use minimal aggregation, fork, exec and exit will
.Em not
be aggregated.
.It Fl H
Enable SHMGET events.
.It Fl h
Display this manpage.
.It Fl K Ar kubeconfig
Enrich events with kubernetes metadata, requires quark-kube-talker.
.Ar kubeconfig
is the path to the kubernetes configuration, usually
.Pa $HOME/.kube/config .
.It Fl k
Attempt kprobe as the backend.
.It Fl L
Enable module loading events.
.It Fl l Ar maxlength
Maximum lenght of the quark queue, essentially how much quark is willing to
buffer, refer to
.Xr quark_queue_open 3
for further details.
.It Fl m Ar maxnodes
Don't really process events, just collect
.Ar maxnodes
in the sorting tree
and output
.Pa quark_by_time.dot
and
.Pa quark_by_pidtime.dot .
The first is events sorted by time, and the second by pid plus time.
Exits after
.Ar maxnodes
has been reached.
This is used purely for internal debugging.
.It Fl M
Run in a simple benchmark form that only counts and display stats.
.It Fl N
Enable DNS events (experimental).
.It Fl n
Attempt NOVA as the backend.
.It Fl P Ar ppid
Display only events where parent pid is
.Ar ppid .
.It Fl S
Enable socket events (experimental).
.It Fl s
Don't send the initial snapshot of existing processes.
.It Fl r Ar rulefile
Read rules from
.Ar rulefile ,
use
.Em -
for stdin.
See
.Sx RULES .
.It Fl T
Enable ptrace event tracing.
.It Fl t
Don't supress thread events, this is only useful for debugging and will likely
be zapped in the future.
.It Fl u
Enable executable mprotect attempt events (EBPF only).
One event is emitted for every VMA that reaches the
.Fn security_file_mprotect
check with effective execute permission.
The event does not assert that the protection change ultimately succeeded.
.It Fl v
Increase verbosity, can be specified multiple times for more verbosity.
.It Fl V
Print version and exit.
.El
.Sh BACKEND SELECTION
If no backend option is specified,
.Nm
uses EBPF.
Exactly one of
.Fl b ,
.Fl k
or
.Fl n
may be passed, there is no fallback between backends.
.Sh RULES
A rule file has one rule per line.
A rule is an action, the word
.Cm on ,
and one or more fields, or the word
.Cm any
for every event:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
drop on process.uid 0 process.exe /usr/bin/*
pass on any
.Ed
.Pp
A
.Sq #
starts a comment that ends at the end of the line.
A value with a blank, a
.Sq # ,
a
.Sq {
or a
.Sq }
must be quoted, as in
.Ql \(dq/opt/my app/*\(dq .
The words of the rule language are reserved:
.Cm pass ,
.Cm drop ,
.Cm poison ,
.Cm on ,
.Cm any ,
.Cm tag ,
.Cm except
and the field names.
A value spelled like one of them must be quoted too, as in
.Ql drop on pod.name \(dqtag\(dq ,
since unquoted it is read as the word and the rule fails to load.
Only the whole value counts, so
.Ql /usr/bin/tag
needs no quotes.
A
.Sq \e
at the end of a line continues the rule on the next line.
.Pp
The actions are:
.Bl -tag -width "poison N"
.It Cm pass
The event is delivered, and the evaluation ends.
.It Cm drop
The event is dropped, unless it has a tag, see
.Sx Tag blocks .
The evaluation ends.
.It Cm poison Ar N
The process of the event gets the poison tag
.Ar N ,
which the
.Cm poison
field matches.
The process keeps it for its later events, and its children inherit it.
The evaluation goes on.
.El
.Pp
All fields of a rule must match.
A field without data for the event, like
.Cm file.path
on a process event, or a container field for a process without container
metadata, does not match.
The fields are:
.Bl -tag -width "container.image"
.It Cm process.pid , process.ppid , process.uid , process.gid , process.sid Ar number
The process id, the parent process id, the user id, the group id and the
session id of the process.
.Cm process.pid 0
and
.Cm process.ppid 0
are rejected.
.It Cm process.exe Ar path
The executable of the process.
.It Cm file.path Ar path
The path of the file of a file event.
.It Cm file.exec_change
A file was created, or its permissions changed, and it is executable
afterwards.
This field takes no value.
.It Cm event.scope Ar container | host | unknown
See
.Sx CONTAINER SCOPE .
.It Cm pod.name Ar name
The name of the Kubernetes pod of the process container.
.It Cm container.image Ar image
The image reference of the process container, exactly as Kubernetes reports
it, or as given to the container API, as in
.Dq container.image docker.io/library/nginx:1.25 .
.It Cm poison Ar N
The process has the poison tag
.Ar N .
.El
.Pp
.Cm process.exe ,
.Cm file.path ,
.Cm pod.name
and
.Cm container.image
accept one
.Sq *
wildcard at any position, it matches any text, including
.Sq / .
So
.Dq file.path /etc/*
matches every file under
.Pa /etc ,
and
.Dq container.image */nginx:1.25
matches that image from any registry.
.Cm pod.name
and
.Cm container.image
require container metadata, from quark-kube-talker, see
.Fl K ,
or from the container API of the library.
.Ss Value lists
A field can take a list of values in braces.
The field matches when one of the values matches:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
drop on process.exe { /bin/* /usr/bin/curl } process.uid { 0 1000 }
.Ed
.Pp
This rule drops an event from root or uid 1000 that runs a program in
.Pa /bin
or curl.
A list may span lines, and an empty list is an error.
.Cm file.exec_change
takes no list.
.Ss Tag blocks
A tag block sets a named tag on the event.
It has
.Cm except on
lines, then
.Cm on
lines, one per line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
drop on event.scope { host unknown }
tag alert {
	except on file.path /tmp/*
	on file.exec_change
}
tag log {
	on file.path *
}
drop on any
.Ed
.Pp
Save the ruleset to
.Pa rules.conf .
To capture file events and apply the ruleset, run
.Dl quark-mon -F -r rules.conf
.Pp
A block sets its tag when no
.Cm except on
line matches and one
.Cm on
line matches.
An
.Cm except on
line only applies to its own block, and it needs a field.
In the example, on a container event, a new executable in
.Pa /tmp
gets the tag
.Sy log ,
a new executable elsewhere gets
.Sy alert
and
.Sy log ,
any other file event gets
.Sy log ,
and an event without a file is dropped.
.Pp
The name is
.Sy log
or
.Sy alert ,
the actions of a Defend for Containers response.
The
.Sq {
follows the name, and the
.Sq }
has a line of its own.
Blocks with the same name are alternatives.
No rule tests a tag.
The default text output prints the tag names, as described in
.Xr quark_event_dump 3 .
With
.Fl E ,
tags still affect event delivery, and the ECS JSON omits them.
.Ss Evaluation
The rules are evaluated from top to bottom.
When a
.Cm pass
or
.Cm drop
rule matches, Quark skips all later rules and tag blocks.
.Cm poison
rules and tag blocks allow evaluation to continue.
Each matching block sets its tag.
.Pp
A matching
.Cm drop
rule drops the event only if it has no tags.
Quark delivers all other events, including events with a tag from an
earlier block and events that match no rule.
.Dq drop on any
as the last rule drops untagged events that reach it.
.Pp
The initial snapshot of existing processes, see
.Fl s ,
is not filtered by the rules.
.Ss Backends
The EBPF backend supports every rule.
The kprobe backend does not report the cgroup of new processes, so it
rejects
.Cm event.scope ,
.Cm pod.name
and
.Cm container.image .
The NOVA backend rejects these three fields,
.Cm file.exec_change ,
value lists with more than one value and tag blocks.
.Sh CONTAINER SCOPE
The
.Cm event.scope
field puts every event in exactly one of three scopes:
.Bl -tag -width "container"
.It Cm container
The last component of the process cgroup is a container scope of the
systemd cgroup driver, like
.Pa cri-containerd-<id>.scope
or
.Pa crio-<id>.scope ,
where
.Ar id
is the container ID in lowercase hex.
This is the kubeadm default since Kubernetes 1.22.
A path relative to a cgroup namespace also identifies a container when
it ends in a recognized container scope, such as
.Pa /../cri-containerd-<id>.scope .
Host units that share the prefix, like the conmon monitor in
.Pa crio-conmon-<id>.scope
or the service
.Pa crio-wipe.service ,
are not containers.
.It Cm host
The process cgroup is an absolute path under
.Pa /init.scope ,
.Pa /system.slice
or
.Pa /user.slice ,
the systemd host layout, with no
.Pa ..
component and no component that is a container scope.
.It Cm unknown
Everything else.
.El
.Pp
To keep only container events on a Kubernetes node, use:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
drop on event.scope { host unknown }
.Ed
.Pp
The following are
.Cm unknown ,
neither a container nor the host, so the rule above drops their events:
.Bl -bullet
.It
All containers on a node that uses the cgroupfs driver, like
.Pa /kubepods/burstable/pod<uid>/<id> .
.It
A process in a nested cgroup of a container, like
.Pa cri-containerd-<id>.scope/init.scope .
.It
The root cgroup
.Pa / ,
any other path relative to a cgroup namespace, such as
.Pa /../system.slice/foo.service ,
and any other layout, such as
.Pa /machine.slice .
.It
An event without a process, or from a process with an unknown or empty
cgroup.
.El
.Pp
Containers outside Kubernetes are not a supported use case.
The field is only supported by the EBPF backend.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 in if a SIGINT was sent, or 1 in case of error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Running:
.Dl $ quark-mon
.Pp
outputs:
